{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"rebar-debug Phase 1 root-cause report (2026-08-10)\n\nHistorical event reconstruction for puerile-defensive-nighthawk shows that the verified 14,997-character description event was not deleted, reverted, or hidden by Git. It remained in the log. A later 10,582-character whole-description EDIT became authoritative, followed by a 15,666-character repair. The REVIEW_RESULT sidecar landed about 0.5 ms before the shorter EDIT but did not overwrite the description.\n\nTwo mechanisms were tested separately:\n\n1. Historical reducer-cache race — proven and already fixed by commit 0b964c82f5 / closed ticket likeminded-flameproof-tick. Old code let concurrent reducer generations share one .cache.json.tmp; an older reducer could publish a current event hash with stale state. A deterministic regression test passes 5/5 on current origin/main and fails on the parent of that fix by returning old-title while cache-bypassing replay returns new-title. This mechanism can explain a successful immediate read-back followed by a later stale read.\n\n2. Stale-base whole-field overwrite — still reproducible on current origin/main. edit --description appends an unconditional complete replacement, and the reducer applies EDIT fields last-writer-wins. In an isolated repository, after writing fresh-B, a later writer submitted stale client text base-plus-A; both normal reduction and cache-bypassing full replay then returned base-plus-A. This is not caching: it is a newer valid event carrying stale client-held data.\n\nThe historical log proves the later writer used older text, but it cannot distinguish whether that stale base came from the now-fixed reducer cache race, a harness retaining old text, or another clone operating inside the documented freshness window. Git caching and filesystem caching are not supported by the evidence: reduction reads current event files, and publication uses atomic rename before the write returns.\n\nDisposition: do not close this as merely a duplicate of the fixed cache ticket. The cache defect is fixed, but stale whole-body writes can still silently clobber newer content. Preventing that requires an explicit API/design decision such as a base revision/fingerprint or field-level compare-and-swap; current optimistic concurrency only covers state-dependent operations, so that behavior should not be changed silently."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786388326118574001,"uuid":"86d7f450-dc6b-427b-8535-09bb5937f30a"}